(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Getty Images Holdings and six affiliates filed voluntary Chapter 11 petitions on 2026-10-21 in the US Bankruptcy Court for the District of Delaware, to carry out a prepackaged plan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Under a Restructuring Support Agreement of 2026-10-12, Getty family entities and Koch Equity Development affiliates committed up to $533 million of new equity; on emergence the company becomes privately held, 100% owned by those investors. A stated plan, not yet confirmed by the court.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"About $1.46 billion of funded debt at the petition date; the plan eliminates more than $500 million of it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Debtor-in-possession financing of up to $143 million from a KED affiliate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"\"No anticipated impact\" on contributors, partners, customers, employees or vendors. A stated intention in a press release, not a finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"A combined hearing on the disclosure statement and plan confirmation on 2026-12-14: requested by the debtors, subject to the court's approval and availability; not a set date.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"The case numbers (26-11643 to 26-11649) and the assigned judge. Press reports only; the court docket and the claims agent's site were not opened. Not on air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-p7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2: Payroll writers and authors (27-3043) went from 54,010 to 47,940, May 2022 to May 2025 (-11.2%); median pay $73,150 to $76,910. Ledger word: PARTLY TRUE that the count fell. Note: 4,560 of the 6,070 fall is already in the May 2023 estimate, which pools survey panels from Nov 2020 to May 2023, so it is mostly pre-ChatGPT data (see A9). Comparator: about 1 in 9 payroll writers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2/U14: Graphic designers (27-1024): 211,890 (2022), 212,720 (2023), 214,260 (2024), 197,830 (2025). The 2023 and 2024 points, which the dossier had only via Adobe, are now confirmed at the BLS file. The whole fall is 2024 to 2025 (-7.7% in one year), two years after the image tools launched. Median $57,990 to $62,960.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2: Fine artists incl. illustrators (27-1013): 12,080 to 11,220 (-7.1%), median $57,560 to $55,490. The 2022 estimate carries a 13.4% relative standard error, so the -7.1% is well inside sampling error (z about -0.5, A7). Ledger word for 'illustrator jobs fell': NOT MEASURED by this series.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U69: Photographers (27-4021): 47,380 to 51,760 (+9.2%), median $40,170 to $44,660. Most of the rise is 2022 to 2023 (to 53,630).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U70: Editors 101,430 to 91,690 (-9.6%; most of it, to 95,700, by May 2023); technical writers 48,620 to 45,500 (-6.4%), median $79,960 to $90,390; interpreters and translators 52,160 to 52,060 (flat), median $53,640 to $60,170.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G16 (new data): the music, voice and screen jobs the dossier lacked. May 2022 to May 2025: musicians and singers 31,750 to 36,180 (+14%); actors 54,160 to 55,000 (RSE 17.7%, noise); broadcast announcers and DJs 26,820 to 21,240 (-20.8%); special effects artists and animators 35,990 to 19,970 (-44.5%); film and video editors 32,080 to 25,610 (-20.2%). BLS publishes no median for musicians or actors. The screen-job falls track the production slump in A17, not any AI measure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G7 (baseline + noise, my arithmetic on the BLS files): all payroll jobs +5.1% over the same three years; the whole arts/design/media group -0.8% (not significant). Against sampling error: writers -11.2% gives z = -1.7, NOT distinguishable from noise at 95%; fine artists z = -0.5 (noise); graphic designers z = -2.8 and editors z = -4.6 (real falls). Real pay (CPI-U +10.0%): writers -4.4%, designers -1.3%, fine artists -12.3%, photographers +1.1%, translators +2.0%, all occupations +0.1%. Non-creative comparator: web developers fell 20.8% (z = -3.5) in the same window, with no image or writing tool involved.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G7 (the prior trend, May 2019): writers were 45,860 in 2019, so 2025's 47,940 is still ABOVE the pre-pandemic level: the 'fall' undoes a 2019-22 rise. Translators fell from 58,870 to 52,160 BEFORE ChatGPT and have been flat since. Announcers were already falling (29,230). Designers were 215,930 (slow decline before AI). Caveat: May 2019 is a hybrid 2010/2018 SOC estimate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2 scriptTrap, BLS's own words: OEWS is not built for year-to-year comparison, pools three years of panels, and excludes the self-employed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2: BLS's AI sentence on writers is a 2025-35 PROJECTION, not a cause of the 2022-25 fall.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2/U14: BLS projects designers -2% for 2025-35 and names AI as a productivity tool that reduces need. Projection only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F4: 'BLS names AI competition only for illustrators' is WRONG. BLS also says AI images will partly replace stock photos (A13) and that AI will dampen demand for writers (A10). Right wording: BLS's projections name AI for illustrators, photographers, writers, designers and technical writers; for illustrators it says 'may compete'. Error came from the engine's finding. Also: OOH counts 28,900 fine-artist jobs incl. self-employed vs 11,220 on payroll.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U69: BLS projects photographers -1% to 2035 and names smartphones first, AI-made stock photos second; it expects self-employed photographers to grow.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U70: Editors: BLS blames print news and magazines; the editors page does not mention AI at all (agent grep: 0 hits for 'artificial intelligence').","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U70/F6: Translators: BLS says AI makes the work more efficient but most jobs 'cannot be entirely automated'; projected +2% to 2035.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U70: Technical writers: BLS says growth 'may be slowed by' AI; projected +1%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U22/F8: CES motion picture and sound recording (thousands): 456.1 (Nov 2022) to 328.5 (Sep 2026), -127.6k. Context the dossier lacked: Nov 2022 was a post-pandemic rebound peak (437.6 in May 2019; 213.3 in May 2020), and the series was 377.5 by Oct 2023, during the strikes. BLS gives no cause. Read from FRED's copy (current vintage), not bls.gov.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U20/F8: '118,500' is a FORECAST of jobs 'likely' affected by 2026, not a count of jobs lost. Ledger word for 'AI has cost 100,000+ film and TV jobs': FALSE as stated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U21/F8: the film/TV sample is n=150, not n=120. The figure in that section cites n=150, and 'n=120' appears nowhere in the report. Possible source of the error: the text's 'Nearly 120,000'. Error came from the engine's finding. Confirmed: 300 executives, 17 Nov to 22 Dec 2023; radio and broadcasting answers folded in; respondents estimated what they 'expect'.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U100/U62: 'disruption' means leaders' expectations over the next three years; jobs AI might create are excluded; 75% said GenAI supported cuts in their division; commissioned by the Animation Guild and three other groups (page 57 lists the Concept Art Association, the Animation Guild, the Human Artistry Campaign and the National Cartoonists Society Foundation).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U12/F21: WEF 2025: 170M created, 92M displaced, net +78M by 2030 (all trends, whole economy); AI and information processing +11M / -9M; employer survey May to Sept 2024; WEF says the conclusions are not comprehensive. Ledger word for 'AI will create more creative jobs than it destroys': NOT MEASURED.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U13: WEF 2023 projected net -14M (69M created, 83M lost); 803 companies; fieldwork Nov 2022 to Feb 2023. So the headline sign flipped between editions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U99: WEF 2025 on graphic designers: just outside the top-10 fastest-declining, hedged 'may illustrate', reversing 2023's 'moderately growing'. Employer expectation, not a measured loss (the measured US fall is A2).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F3/U46: Hui, Reshef & Zhou (writing): 2% fewer monthly jobs and 5.2% lower monthly earnings vs less-affected freelancers; 1.2% less likely to get any job; 92,547 freelancers; a snapshot of profiles as of April 2023. Read in the SSRN working-paper version; the published Organization Science text was not opened. Ledger word: TRUE on the platform, relative to a comparison group.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F4/U46: Hui et al. (image): -3.7% jobs and -9.4% income (Table A3); the 7% and 14% apply only to above-median prior earners; the top-earner heterogeneity is 'often not statistically significant' for ChatGPT.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U46 says Demirci et al. used 'about 2 million postings'. WRONG: the paper uses 1,386,642 job posts from one unnamed global platform, July 2021 to July 2023. Error came from a secondary relay (the WashU news item the claim cites). Results confirmed: -21% for automation-prone posts within eight months; writing -30.37%; graphic design -18.49%; 3D modelling -15.57%. Read in the working-paper version.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F3/F4: Liu et al. (arXiv preprint): exposed text submarkets show a 39.40% relative fall in transaction value, on submarkets averaging about $2,400 a month; image submarkets: no significant effects.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U82/F3: Teutloff et al. Three errors in the dossier. (1) 'About Us' page writing fell 59% (real-estate writing 52%), not 50%. (2) Western-European translation fell 23%, not 30%. (3) The fall was NOT 'hardest for experienced workers': the paper finds limited experience differences for substitutable skills and a fall for NOVICE workers in complementary skills. The engine's finding (via the ETLA summary) caused all three. Confirmed: 20-50% vs trend, sharpest for 1-3 week jobs, ML +24%, chatbots nearly tripled. Key context: substitutable postings fell 7% in raw terms while unaffected postings ROSE 17%; the -24/25% is the gap between them.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U30/U32/F5/G13: SoA, now read at the primary. 26% of illustrators and 36% of translators who answered 'have already lost work due to generative AI'; 787 responses from 12,500 members and other authors, January 2024. The dossier says the translator subsample is unpublished. WRONG: the SoA post's author account says 78 respondents were translators, so 36% is about 28 people. The illustrator count and the question wording are still unpublished. The error came from the engine and secondary relays, which never opened the page.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U61/G9: Authors Guild: 6% aware of losing work to AI, 50% don't know, 2,400+ authors. Page gives no recruitment method or question text. The 'don't know' option is the documented wording difference from SoA.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U63/G13: NAVA 2026: 21% said yes to the exact question 'Have you ever knowingly lost a job to a synthetic voice?' (read from the chart image, transcribed). The prior year's 14% was NOT checked (2025 survey page not opened).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U63: NAVA recruitment and size: 1,379 responses, 22 Jan to 22 Feb 2026, unsponsored social posts and voiceover Facebook groups, so self-selected.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F6: ELIS 2025: half of language companies reported revenue down, by 19% on average among them; 18% reported it up, by 16%; 654 independents; 23% of independents considering ending.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U31/F6: Frey & Llanos-Paredes. The Oxford Martin page confirms that areas with more Google Translate use saw a DECLINE in translator employment (it says 'decline', the dossier says 'fewer added'). The '28,000' and 'not mass displacement' come from CNN and are not on this page: secondary-verified only. It concerns machine translation, not generative AI.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U76/F8: FilmLA ties the LA filming drop to global cutbacks and rival territories; this Q1 2025 release has 0 AI mentions (agent grep). The dossier's annual '19,694 shoot days, -16%' comes from a different release, not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U17/F22: Noy & Zhang, checked against the ABSTRACT only (PubMed; Science full text paywalled): 453 professionals, 40% less time, 18% higher quality, inequality fell. The dossier's 'did not fact-check accuracy' is not in the abstract (unverified).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U18/F22: Doshi & Hauser: N = 293 writers recruited on Prolific; novelty +5.4% / +8.1%, usefulness +3.7% / +9.0%; AI-aided stories more alike (10.7% and 8.9% of the similarity range).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U73/F22 (the abstract-wording sample): Zhou & Lee's abstract says +25% productivity, but the body says +50% on adoption, doubling the next month. The '2% to 3%' favourites-per-view, the falling average novelty and the 50,000+ users all check out. Say 'roughly a quarter more output, per the abstract' and keep the body's bigger number off air, or quote both. It measures posting volume and likes, not income.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a38"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U74: Figma Make trial: 50 designers plus 50 PMs, about 20% shorter times among those who finished, larger gains for PMs (abstract). The ISR design study and the BCG numbers were not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a39"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U44: Prompt engineer: 72 of 20,662 LinkedIn postings, under 0.5% (abstract). The Indeed '3 in 1,000' figure was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a40"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U88/F3/F21: Upwork, a company press release relayed by Quiver: gen-AI and creative-production contract starts +90% with per-contract earnings -13%; AI freelancers earn 34% more per hour; based on a 2,400-person survey plus marketplace data. The 109% and 329% figures were not checked. Company-reported.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a41"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U54/G17: Makridis, read in the author's own ACEI post (the journal article and the CESifo PDF are behind bot checks): no early earnings collapse for more AI-exposed artistic jobs; employment 'more ambiguous'; hours respond more. This is the best honest 'no harm yet' row. The occupation list was not seen.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a42"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F2 verdict wording: 'The government's own job numbers show AI wiping out creative jobs.' The ledger word stays PARTLY TRUE only for 'payroll counts fell for some creative jobs'. Correct the evidence: it is not 'writers, designers and fine artists fell' as one story. Writers fell by an amount within sampling error and are still above 2019 (A7, A8). The fine-artist fall is noise (A3). Designers fell significantly, but only in 2024-25 (A2). Musicians and photographers rose (A4, A6). The whole creative group is flat (-0.8%) while all jobs grew 5.1% (A7). BLS attributes none of the change to AI. Error came from the engine reading point estimates without standard errors.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a43"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F6 scriptTrap: the '78,000 to 74,000' translator line has no BLS payroll basis. Payroll translators were 58,870 in 2019 and 52,060 in 2025, so most of the decline predates ChatGPT (A8). The dossier's 'flat to slightly down' since 2022 stands; 'AI killed translation' is FALSE as a collapse.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a44"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F1: \"AI bankrupted Getty Images.\" In Getty's own account (an interested party's filing to its creditors and the court), three non-operating events caused the filing: the warrant litigation, the merger fees and debt, and the debt service. Getty says the iStock and agency trends would not on their own have forced the filing. LTM revenue to Q2 2026 was about $978M. Ledger: FALSE as the cause; PARTLY TRUE as a headwind on one brand.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F1: in the FY2025 10-K, revenue rose from $916.6M (2023) to $939.3M (2024) to $981.3M (2025, +4.5%). Creative fell from $578.7M (63.1%) to $552.8M (58.9%), then edged up to $556.9M (56.7%). \"Other\" grew from 1.9% to 5.6% and includes data/AI licensing alongside music, DAM, distribution and print; its 2025 growth was \"primarily driven by data access and/or licensing\". Net loss was $206.2M in 2025, against net income of $39.5M in 2024. No going-concern or \"substantial doubt\" wording appears anywhere in this 10-K (search returned 0 hits).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U11/U33 + G14: \"The 835,000 to 636,000 fall is company-wide, not iStock-only.\" WRONG AS FRAMED, and so is the opposite reading. The disclosure introduces the series with \"Within these areas\" (iStock and agency) and says the metrics \"reflect conditions specific to the iStock and agency customer groups\". But its numbers are the same company-wide KPI that the 10-Ks report (799k for 2023, 717k for 2024; see B4). Right wording: \"Getty's count of paying customers fell about a quarter, from about 835,000 in 2022 to about 636,000 in the year to June 2026. Getty says that count is driven mostly by its budget iStock brand and its agency customers, and that is where it says the fall happened.\" Error source: engine finding (via the Bondoro deck). The peg hunt's \"iStock and agency metrics only\" is Getty's framing, not the metric's definition.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G14: the pre-2026 per-year customer counts no longer rest on a search snippet. The FY2024 10-K gives 835k (2022), 799k (2023) and 717k (2024), and defines the metric as ALL customers who made a purchase.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G11 (non-AI causes, FY2023): Getty's own 10-K explains the 2023 customer fall by the shift to subscriptions and shrinking agency customers. It does not mention AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G11 (non-AI causes, FY2024): the 2024 customer fall is explained by lower a-la-carte volume as customers moved to annual subscriptions, and revenue per customer rose. It does not mention AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G11 (non-AI causes, FY2025): the 2025 customer fall is explained by iStock subscriptions and by the end of a free-trial acquisition programme in June 2025. Revenue per purchasing customer rose 8.7% to $1,424. It does not mention AI. Only the later bankruptcy filing adds generative AI to the list of causes (B10).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U11 + F1: in its own words, Getty says generative AI pressured the budget microstock market, \"a small portion\" of its business. It also says it licenses images, video and metadata to AI companies for training, which it calls \"a growing new revenue stream\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U34: the non-operating figures, all confirmed at the filing itself (not only the deck). Over $200M of judgments plus interest, $35M of legal fees, a $99.5M reserve, over $275M of unasserted warrant claims, over $100M of merger fees, about $10M of SOX costs, $43.6M of net interest/fees on the merger notes, and $1.46B of funded debt. Trap: \"about $154M of interest a year\" and \"about $239M of annual debt service\" are two different figures in the same filing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U33/U34 wording: \"AI is felt 'mainly in iStock and among agency customers'\". That phrase is NOT in the filing (it is the deck's paraphrase). The filing's actual words are \"felt principally in the Company's iStock brand ... and among its agency customers\". AI is also one of SEVERAL structural shifts it names, alongside changes in media consumption and marketing strategies. The heading is \"Broader Industry Headwinds\". Error source: secondary relay (Bondoro deck).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G14 (going concern): the FY2025 10-K (filed 2026-03-16) carries no going-concern language (0 hits for \"going concern\" or \"substantial doubt\"). The October disclosure statement says management later concluded there was substantial doubt, after the warrant judgment and the April 2026 revolver draw. This is a 2026 development, not a 10-K finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U84 / F1: \"Getty walked away on 30 June 2026.\" Getty publicly announced the termination on June 30. The formal notice that ended the agreement was delivered on July 7, 2026. The CMA's final report of May 15, 2026 required Shutterstock's entire editorial business to be sold. Error source: engine finding (via the deck).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U84 + G11: the one independent (regulator) voice. The CMA found no stock-content competition concern because GenAI firms are \"likely\" to \"increasingly compete\" with Getty and Shutterstock, and Adobe and Canva will add pressure. This is a forward-looking competition finding, not a measured loss of sales. Read at the CMA's own news release of 2026-07-07; the final-report PDF itself was not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U10: contributors were paid over $220M of royalties in 2025, from all sources. Getty says its AI models \"compensate creators on a recurring basis\" for training data but never gives an amount. The AI share of the $220M is undisclosed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G11 (royalties over time, 2023): \"nearly $220 million\" in 2023. Total contributor royalties were therefore flat, at nearly, nearly and over $220M across 2023-2025, while revenue grew. Per-contributor earnings cannot be computed: Getty gives only exclusive-contributor counts and says it distributes \"over 600,000 contributors\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G11 (royalties and contributors, 2024): nearly $220M in royalties; over 81,000 exclusive contributors (the FY2025 10-K says over 83,000; the disclosure says over 84,000).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U71: Shutterstock revenue rose from $874.6M to $935.3M to $989.9M (2023-2025, per the 10-K table). Content went from $737.3M to $760.0M to $786.7M, and Data, Distribution and Services from $137.3M to $175.3M to $203.3M. The 10-K says organic Content revenue \"has declined\" and growth came from acquisitions and DDS. Correction to \"led by data licensing\": DDS also includes Giphy distribution/advertising and Shutterstock Studios custom content, so it is not all data licensing. Shutterstock's contributor payout total is not disclosed in the text read.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F10: \"Half of new music on streaming is AI.\" TRUE only as UPLOADS, at ONE service (Deezer), on PEAK days in June 2026. The body of the same release says \"monthly average of 90,000\", which contradicts \"nearly 90,000 ... every day\". The release also dates its first tagging both June 2025 and June 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F10: as LISTENING, AI is 1-3% of Deezer streams. \"Up to 85%\" of streams ON fully-AI tracks were fraudulent in 2025, against 8% fraud catalogue-wide. Deezer also counted 13.4M AI tracks in 2025.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G10 (detector): the dossier says Deezer gives \"no false-positive rate\". That is wrong. Deezer publishes a SELF-REPORTED accuracy of 99.8% and a false-positive rate under 1 in 10,000, and it sells the tool. No independent audit or third-party test was found or opened. Right wording: \"Deezer says its detector is 99.8% accurate; nobody outside Deezer has published a test of it.\" Error source: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G10 (harm to human artists): \"AI music is stealing human artists' royalties.\" At Deezer, Deezer itself says the opposite: fraudulent streams are removed from the pool, so the impact on human artists' royalties is \"minimal\". The only harm figure in the release is CISAC/PMP's FORECAST of creators' revenue \"at risk by 2028\", not a measured loss. Ledger: NOT MEASURED as a realised loss industry-wide; Deezer's own claim is minimal impact on Deezer. Error source: engine finding (\"no source shows payouts fell\" understated it, because the source says payouts were protected).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G18 (Deezer 2025 points, read at Deezer): January 2025 launch, about 10,000 a day, about 10% of deliveries, up to 70% of AI-track streams fraudulent. Trap: this newsroom page has been edited since, and it now cites July 2026 figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G18: April 2025, over 20,000 a day, over 18% of uploads.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G18: September 2025, over 30,000 a day, over 28% of deliveries. AI tracks were then about 0.5% of streams, so the listening share also grew, from about 0.5% (Sept 2025) to 1-3% (June 2026). Implied Deezer total daily deliveries: about 100k (Jan 2025) and about 107k (Sept 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U35: pro-rata pool. CONFIRMED in the DOJ's own description: fraud diverts money from one proportional pool. UNRESOLVED: Deezer's \"tens of millions of euros diverted\" and the 1,000-stream user cap. Neither appears in any of the four Deezer releases opened (Jan, Apr, Sept 2025; July 2026); they rest on trade relay.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U85 + G18 (Luminate comparator): 106,000 new ISRCs a day delivered to streaming services in 2025, 253M tracks at year end, 120.5M with under ten streams. Secondary-verified: MBW quoting Luminate's report; the Luminate PDF was not opened. Scope trap: Deezer's implied total deliveries in 2025 (about 100-107k a day, B22/B24) are about the size of Luminate's industry-wide figure. So \"90k AI a day vs 106k all tracks\" sets a June-2026 peak at one service against a 2025 industry average. It must not be read as \"almost all new music is AI\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G10 (second platform): Spotify removed over 75M \"spammy tracks\" in 12 months. This is a spam count, not an AI count: Spotify publishes no AI share of uploads. Spotify's payouts grew from $1B (2014) to $10B (2024).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F11/U48: \"He faced 60 years.\" Only as three stacked 20-year counts in the 2024 indictment. Now read at the DOJ itself.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U48: he pleaded to ONE count with a 5-year maximum and agreed to forfeit $8,091,843.64. The release does not say what happened to the other two counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F11/U49: sentence on 2026-10-06. 18 months, 2 years of supervised release, $8,091,843.64 forfeiture; plea dated March 19, 2026. Now read at DOJ (primary), not only at blogs. Trap: the October release's headline says \"Super Intelligence-Assisted\", where the March release said \"artificial intelligence\". Quote the facts, not the headline.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U49: guidelines 46-57 months; government asked for 46, probation recommended 24; the judge called the range \"somewhat inflated\". Secondary-verified: Inner City Press's courtroom account. MBW agrees on 46-57, 46 and 24. No transcript or judgment opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U50: \"$10M vs $8.09M is not a contradiction.\" It is the DOJ's OWN revision: \"more than $10 million\" at charging (2024), \"more than $8 million\" at plea and sentence (2026). The forfeiture is $8,091,843.64. Say \"about $8 million\", and if the $10M appears, attribute it to the 2024 charge.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b32"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U104/U108: \"He's punished, the platforms aren't.\" The platforms are the defrauded parties in DOJ's framing, and the money came out of the shared pool. Prosecutors sought no restitution. The judge said AI use was not the target. A prosecutor said a platform flagged him in 2018 (the dossier's \"several times\" is not in what I read; MBW separately reports that The MLC halted his publishing payments in 2023). Secondary-verified at Inner City Press.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b33"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U104: in the DOJ's own words, the scheme defrauded \"music streaming platforms and musicians\". The victims named are platforms AND artists, not platforms alone.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b34"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F14/U53: Pinterest flooding, Garbage Day spot-check (2025-04-22). 12 of 16 of Pinterest's own reported trends showed multiple AI images in the top five, all 16 had them in the top 20, and none were labelled. This is a hand spot-check of trend queries, not a platform-wide count.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b35"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F14/U52/U53: Futurism (Feb 2025) found at least 6 of the first 12 \"healthy recipe ideas\" pins were AI, with 1 labelled. Pinterest's only figure is \"a small percentage\" of impressions (a different base, no number).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b36"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U52 + G18: Pinterest's October 2025 controls post. Its 57% is about \"all online material\" and carries a footnote marker whose source is not shown on the page. Pinterest gives no share for its own feed and says only that categories like beauty, art, fashion and home decor are \"highly prone\". Ledger: NOT MEASURED platform-wide. No 2026 recount was found, and I ran no count of my own (no browser count in scope).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b37"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U89: Adobe Stock. 47.85% of the image portfolio was AI as of 2025-04-08, up from 2.5% in May 2023. Secondary relay of one stock photographer's own count; method not shown. It is a share of LISTINGS, not of sales or downloads. Now 19 months old.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b38"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U95: the viral \"79% of images on Instagram/TikTok/Pinterest are AI\" figure. It traces only to a VC blog attributing it to an AI-detector vendor's \"2026 analysis\", with no sample, tool or period given, and no vendor report was found. The same page repeats the old \"90% of online content by 2026\" projection from Europol's Innovation Lab. Ledger: FALSE as a measured fact / NOT MEASURED. NewsGuard's detector test (13.33%) was not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b39"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U3/F25: the July 2026 working paper (arXiv 2607.20349, v3 dated 3 Aug 2026, \"Working Paper Under Review\", not peer reviewed). It covers 14,419 self-published genre-fiction books on Amazon. Selling titles grew 19.2-fold while revenue grew 8.9-fold, so revenue per selling book fell. No book in the sample disclosed AI use.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b40"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U3/F25: share and causation. Books with more than 25% AI-flagged text were 20% of the sample but 12.1% of sales and 11.3% of revenue, and their sales share rose from near zero to about 20% by 2026 Q2. The dossier says \"the authors say this does not prove causation\", which understates them. They call the comparisons \"observational\" but argue the per-book revenue fall appears even among no-AI books and is \"unlikely to be caused by a market-wide trend\". Their title asserts AI \"floods and dilutes\" the market. Sales come from a proprietary panel, and AI status from one commercial detector (Pangram v3.3). Error source: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b41"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U2: Bowker/PW. Self-published output was up 38.7% in 2025 to more than 3.5M, from 2.5M in 2024, after FALLING in 2024 from 2023. It is up 43.5% from 2022 to 2025, against 10% for traditional (642,242 in 2025, from 602,428). The exact 2022 and 2023 self-published counts (2,459,367 and 2,637,565) are NOT in the article text read; they may sit in a table or image. Bowker's spokesperson credits the rise to self-publishing tools and multi-format editions and does not name AI. ISBN counts cannot identify AI books.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b42"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U1: the KDP cap is up to 2 new titles created and 2 submitted per format per week, resetting Sundays 00:00 UTC (confirmed at Amazon). Amazon's stated reason is \"a positive experience for all authors and readers\", not the dossier's \"a positive publishing experience\", and it gives no AI reason. The 2026-09-21 start, the prior \"ten\" limit and the Sept 2023 \"3 a day\" cap are secondary (ALLi/Vappingo; not re-read).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b43"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U96: KDP requires disclosure of AI-GENERATED (not AI-assisted) content TO AMAZON (\"inform us\"). AI-generated still counts as AI-generated even after substantial edits. The page says nothing about showing it to shoppers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b44"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U86: impostor books (Aug 2023, a publishing-industry author). Half a dozen books under her name; Amazon first asked for trademark numbers, removed them after a backlash, and would not name the rule broken. The 2024 fake-biography case and the NPR \"limiting summaries\" quote were not opened (secondary).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b45"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U35 (Deezer's 1,000-stream cap / \"tens of millions of euros\"): not found in any Deezer primary opened. Must not air unless read at Deezer. The only Deezer € figure is the CISAC forecast (B21).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b46"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U7/F18: Copyright Office Part 2 (Jan 2025): purely AI-generated material is not copyrightable. Prompts alone don't give enough control with current tools. Human-authored elements, and human selection, arrangement or modification, are protectable case by case. Agency report, not a court ruling.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U8: Thaler v. Perlmutter (D.C. Cir., decided 18 Mar 2025): the Copyright Act requires a human author. The ruling does not bar works made with AI help. Thaler told the Office the work was autonomously generated, and his own-authorship argument was held waived, so the case does not decide AI-assisted works.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U8: the Supreme Court declined review in Thaler (No. 25-449, cert denied 2 Mar 2026), read at the Court's own docket.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U72: Zarya of the Dawn (Copyright Office letter of 21 Feb 2023): the author's text and her selection, coordination and arrangement are protected. The Midjourney images are not, and a prompter does not \"actually form\" them. Small wording fix: the letter says the Office intends to cancel and reissue the registration; it does not report that this already happened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U90/F18: Li v Liu (Beijing Internet Court, judgment of 27 Nov 2023, read in the court's own English translation): the prompter is the author and owns the copyright in a Stable Diffusion image. Damages were 500 yuan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U90: the \"150+ prompts\" detail and a caveat the dossier lacked, both from the US Copyright Office's own account of Li v Liu. The ruling is NOT precedential in Chinese practice, so \"China's courts have gone the other way\" overstates it: one internet court, in one case.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U97/F16: Part 3 (pre-publication, May 2025) says copyright does not protect style, that stylistic imitation can still count as market harm, and that copying a \"style\" can sometimes capture protectable elements. Wording fix: the Office's own sentence says \"a popular Japanese animation studio\". The name Ghibli appears only in the title of a cited Fast Company article in footnote 378. \"Cites the Ghibli trend\" is fine; \"names Ghibli\" is loose.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G3 (Japan, answers F16's trap): Japan Copyright Office / Agency for Cultural Affairs, \"General Understanding on AI and Copyright in Japan\" (May 2024, overview). Under Art. 30-4, output that copies only a style (an idea) does not infringe. But the training exception may not apply to training that \"picks off\" a specific creator's style from a small set of their works. The document says it is not legally binding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G3: CODA (Japan's Content Overseas Distribution Association, whose members include Studio Ghibli) wrote to OpenAI in late Oct 2025 asking it not to train on members' works without permission, and rejected the opt-out approach. Secondary-verified (TechCrunch, 3 Nov 2025). The letter itself was not opened, and Ghibli has filed no lawsuit in the record.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U105/F17: Part 3 conclusions. Training uses are likely transformative, and fairness depends on the works, the source, the purpose and the output controls. Commercial mass use to make competing content, \"especially\" through illegal access, goes beyond established fair use. Government intervention would be premature. Agency view, pre-publication, not law.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U68/F17: Bartz v. Anthropic, Order on Fair Use (N.D. Cal., Judge Alsup, 23 Jun 2025). Training was \"exceedingly transformative\" fair use. Scanning purchased print books was fair use, for a different reason.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U68: same order. The pirated central-library copies were not fair use, and a trial was set on them.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U68 settlement, read at the final approval order (Dkt 680, Judge Martínez-Olguín, filed 20 Jul 2026). $1.5B fund. 482,460 works on the list, with 440,490 claimed by 16 Apr 2026. About $3,000 per work \"less costs and fees\", split among copyright owners (author and publisher) by default splits or by contract. Fees $101,561,111. The release covers past inputs only and excludes output claims and conduct from 25 Aug 2025 on.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U6/F17: Kadrey v. Meta (N.D. Cal., Judge Chhabria, 25 Jun 2025, Dkt 598). Meta won partial summary judgment on fair use only because these 13 plaintiffs offered no market-dilution evidence. The judge says the ruling does not make Meta's training lawful, and that in most cases training without permission will likely be illegal. Not a class action.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F17 (error in the finding): \"US district courts have twice found training on LAWFULLY OBTAINED books fair use\" and \"pirated acquisition lost\" are wrong for Kadrey. Meta downloaded from shadow libraries (LibGen, Anna's Archive), and Chhabria held the downloading itself transformative, citing Bartz with \"contra\". Right wording: two judges in the same district split on pirated copies. Bartz said pirated library copies were not fair use. Kadrey said downloading pirated books for transformative training can be fair use, while bad faith stays relevant. Source of the error: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U6: the follow-on order of 27 Jun 2025 (Dkt 601) granted Meta summary judgment on the DMCA claim because the copying was deemed fair use.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U6: the torrenting/distribution claim is still live, with a summary-judgment hearing set for 25 Feb 2027. Secondary-verified (ChatGPT Is Eating the World, 27 Mar 2026, quoting the scheduling order). The docket itself was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U39/F17: Thomson Reuters v. Ross (3rd Cir., No. 25-2153, argued 11 Jun 2026, filed 29 Sep 2026; Montgomery-Reeves with Restrepo and Bove). The court affirmed that 2,243 headnotes are copyrightable and that ROSS's use was not fair. It weighed harm to the original market, to the value of the work, and to the derivative market for licensing headnotes as AI training data. The \"three markets\" wording is approximately right (the court lists original market, value, derivative market, plus public benefit). The court itself calls it \"no more than an ordinary copyright case\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U39: ROSS was NOT generative, and the court distinguished the generative-AI cases (Bartz, In re OpenAI) by name.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U64: the US government (DOJ) filed a statement of interest in In re OpenAI (S.D.N.Y., MDL 1:25-md-3143) on 1 Sep 2026 arguing that LLM training is transformative. Confirmed via the 3rd Circuit's own footnote 7. The phrase \"extraordinarily transformative\" was NOT checked in the filing itself.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U64: In re OpenAI (SDNY, Judge Stein) has no fair-use ruling. Summary-judgment replies are due 6 Nov 2026 and no hearing date is set. Secondary-verified (ChatGPT Is Eating the World, 24 Mar 2026). The docket itself was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U64: AAP/STM/IBPA/CCC amicus (AAP page dated 29 Oct 2026) says an AI-training licensing market exists, at $5.2B in 2025 and a projected $55.8B by 2035. This is an ADVOCACY figure in a party-side brief, verified only as what AAP says.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U94: Concord v. Anthropic (N.D. Cal., Judge Eumi Lee). Cross-motions on fair use for 499 songs were heard 21 Oct 2026, with no ruling. Secondary-verified (aimusicpreneur.com, a trade blog). The separate Jan 2026 suit (20,000+ songs, $3B+) is not in this source and is unchecked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U26: Getty v Stability ([2025] EWHC 2863 (Ch), High Court, Joanna Smith J, 4 Nov 2025). The model does not store or reproduce Getty works, so it is not an \"infringing copy\", although an intangible model can be an \"article\". The secondary infringement claim failed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U26: the training claim was abandoned because there was no evidence the training happened in the UK, and the outputs claim was abandoned because the prompts had been blocked. UK courts have NOT ruled on whether training infringes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U27: trade marks. Narrow wins on iStock watermarks (v1.x via DreamStudio/Developer Platform) and on a Getty watermark (v2.1), based on specific example images, with scale unknowable. The SD XL/v1.6 claims and the s.10(3) claim were dismissed. Findings \"historic and extremely limited in scope\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"NEW (U26 context): Getty was granted permission to appeal the secondary-infringement ruling (Dec 2025), so the UK holding is under appeal. Secondary-verified (Burges Salmon). No Court of Appeal ruling was found.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U55/U66: GEMA v OpenAI (LG München I, 42 O 14139/24, 11 Nov 2025), nine songs. Memorisation in the model and output of the lyrics are reproductions outside the TDM exception, and OpenAI is responsible for the outputs, not the users. Personality-rights claims were dismissed, and the ruling is not final. Nuance missing from the dossier: the court said TDM DOES cover the copies made to compile the training corpus; only memorisation inside the model falls outside it. Secondary-verified (LTO, a German legal news site). The judgment and the court's press release were not opened. On 11 Nov OpenAI said it disagreed and was \"considering further steps\". I could not confirm at a primary that an appeal was lodged, so \"OpenAI has said it will appeal\" (U66) stays unconfirmed.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U66: GEMA says it has offered an AI licensing model since Sep 2024. This is GEMA's own claim (advocacy), and GEMA is the plaintiff.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U56/U28: Kneschke v LAION (OLG Hamburg, 5 U 104/24, 10 Dec 2025). The appeal was dismissed: the download was covered by TDM (s.44b) and research (s.60d), and a natural-language reservation was not machine-readable in 2021. A further appeal to the BGH was allowed. The court expressly did NOT decide whether training generative models itself is TDM. Secondary-verified (Technology's Legal Edge, a law-firm blog). The judgment was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U57/F20: the GPAI Code of Practice copyright chapter (Commission's own PDF), Measure 1.3. Signatories commit to crawlers that follow robots.txt (RFC 9309) and other machine-readable reservation protocols, and to publish crawler information.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U57: the Commission's training-content summary template lists the top 10% of scraped domains (SMEs: top 5% or 1,000, whichever is lower) and has a section on respecting TDM reservations.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U78/F20: the Commission's page says the Code is voluntary and lists 22 signatories, including Amazon, Anthropic, Google, IBM, Microsoft, Mistral AI, OpenAI and Cohere. xAI signed only the Safety and Security chapter. Meta is not listed. The July 2025 Meta executive statement was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U77/F20: GPAI duties have applied since 2 Aug 2025, Commission enforcement including fines starts 2 Aug 2026, and pre-existing models must comply by 2 Aug 2027. (The Digital Omnibus 2026/1744 claim was not checked.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U28/F20: Duke study (IMC '25; 130 self-declared bots, 40 days, 36 websites the authors control). Main finding: compliance falls as robots.txt directives get stricter.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U28/F20 (abstract vs body): \"AI search crawlers rarely check robots.txt\" comes from the ABSTRACT. The paper's own results (RQ2) rank AI search crawlers and AI assistants just behind SEO crawlers as the MOST compliant categories, and the authors call that \"surprising\". The least compliant are headless browsers and \"other\". Right wording: one university's sites found weaker compliance with stricter rules, and many bots never or rarely re-checked robots.txt; it did NOT find that AI crawlers ignore it. Separately, the \"60.0% of reputable news sites\" figure is NOT in this paper; its source was not identified and it must not be attributed to the Duke study. Source of the error: the source's own abstract wording, relayed by the engine.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U29: UK government report (published 18 Mar 2026). It dropped the broad exception with opt-out as its preferred way forward, will gather more evidence, and will not intervene in the licensing market for now. It sketches a \"focused exception\" only as illustrative, so \"named no replacement\" is fair.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c37"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U58/F20: Meta's commercial-terms dispute. Complaints reached EU and UK antitrust authorities, Meta denies the terms give unlimited rights, the effective date moved from 30 Oct to 20 Nov, and Meta says robots.txt still works. Secondary-verified (Marketing-Interactive, citing Reuters). The EBU's 28 Oct statement and the CMA's \"urgent discussions\" are NOT in this source and are unchecked. New detail: the Commission has sent requests for information to companies that have not published their training summaries.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c38"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G4: Andersen v. Stability AI (N.D. Cal., Judge Orrick, the visual artists' class action) has NO merits ruling. It is in discovery. The summary-judgment hearing moved from 4 Nov 2026 to 17 Feb 2027, and a later order pushed the schedule about three more months. Secondary-verified (ChatGPT Is Eating the World). The docket itself was not opened. No US court has ruled on fair use for image-generator training.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c39"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G2/F18: the Copyright Office says that since its March 2023 guidance it has registered \"hundreds\" of works that include AI-generated material, covering the human contribution. So \"AI art can't be copyrighted\" does not stop registration of AI-assisted works. No exact count is published in Part 2.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c40"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U40/F7: The strike began 26 July 2024. Confirmed at the union's own release. The same release gives the comparator for the 95.04% figure: a separate strike-authorisation vote of 98.32% in Sept 2023.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U40: The strike was suspended at noon PT on 11 June 2025. That is 320 days from 26 July 2024 (my arithmetic).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U9/U40/F7: Ratified 95.04% to 4.96% on 9 July 2025, which ended the strike, 348 days after it began (my arithmetic). The union itself calls it an \"11-month strike\". The release gives no raw count and no turnout. Comparator: the union DID publish a return (19.25%) for its 2026 TV/theatrical vote (D1-12). Ledger \"it ran about a year\": TRUE (11 months).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U42/F7: Which companies were struck. CORRECTED: the union named TEN companies, not eleven. There is no separate \"Epic Games\" entry (the Epic-side signatory is Llama Productions, which the union calls a \"Fortnite signatory\" in D1-10). The listed name is \"Take 2 Productions Inc.\", not \"Take-Two Interactive\". The error came from a secondary relay (the Wikipedia/Game Developer lists the engine read).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U42: The contract that took effect lists NINE companies. VoiceWorks, which was in the struck group, is not on the ratification release's list. Why is not stated (not researched).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U42/F7 \"it couldn't reach everyone\": Some union work went on through the strike. From day one, any game could hire union talent under three other agreements, including an interim LOCALIZATION agreement. So the dossier's \"a November 2024 localization agreement\" is APPROXIMATE: a localisation route existed from July 2024, and the November 2024 date was not checked. The claim that about 80 games signed interim deals was NOT found in any saved text (UNRESOLVED part).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U42: The new terms carry over to interim-IMA signers but NOT to tiered-budget independent agreements. Games already in production kept going under \"side letter six\", with GTA VI as the example. All per the trade press, citing a union spokesperson. HoYoverse's absence is implied by the lists in D1-4 and D1-5; its separate action was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U41: The 2025 IMA's AI terms, at the union's own summary. Real-time generation pays at least 750% of scale (7.5x) \"per Digital Replica per Interactive Program\". Usage report within 90 days of release. For a struck title, consent is suspended AUTOMATICALLY (elsewhere it takes written notice). Consent must be written and separately signed. ADDITIONS: the AI terms start \"prospectively\", about 90 days after ratification, not on ratification day. The summary has no term on using performances as AI TRAINING data (a search of the extraction for \"train\" found nothing). The contract runs to 31 Oct 2028. Primary is the PDF; the graphicpolicy relay is no longer needed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U41: Pay rises are 15.17% on ratification, then 3% in each of Nov 2025, 2026 and 2027, compounded (the summary says compounded).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F7 ledger claim \"the strike won\". VERDICT: PARTLY TRUE. It won consent, disclosure and pay floors for replicas (D1-8), plus raises. It did NOT win a training-data term (D1-8), and it left out tiered-budget indies (D1-7). During the strike the union itself charged an employer over an AI-voiced character. Comparator: the 2026 TV/theatrical deal did get a training-licence notice term (D1-12).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U98: \"The SAG-AFTRA 2023 TV/theatrical contract regulates replicas but not training\". CORRECTED: OUT OF DATE. The 2023 contract has been replaced. Members ratified a 2026 TV/Theatrical Agreement in June 2026, and it took effect 1 July 2026. Source of the error: the engine read only the 2023 pages.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U98 (current state): The 2026 TV/theatrical deal passed 91.42% on a 19.25% return and runs 1 July 2026 to 30 June 2030. On TRAINING, it only requires a producer who is PAID to license covered performances for training a commercial AI system to notify the union and discuss it. That is not a ban and not a consent right. It also brings a principle favouring human performers over synthetics, and consent for AI dubbing on films starting photography from 1 July 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U98 (WGA part): Confirmed for the 2023 WGA MBA, at the guild's own summary: AI can't write or rewrite, AI output isn't source material, AI can't be required, AI material must be disclosed, and training is only \"reserved\". Whether a 2026 WGA contract has replaced it was NOT checked: the 2023 SAG deal was replaced in 2026 (D1-11), so check the WGA's current contract before saying \"the writers' contract says\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U79/F19: Tennessee ELVIS Act, at the enacted text. \"Voice\" covers a simulation, and there is civil liability for distributing a tool whose \"primary purpose\" is producing a specific person's voice or likeness. In force 1 July 2024. NOTE: it protects any INDIVIDUAL, not only performers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U79: The ELVIS Act passed 93-0 and 30-0, was signed (03/26/2024 on the bill page), and is Public Chapter 588.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U79/G5: California AB 2602 (Labor Code 927, Ch. 259, signed 17 Sept 2024). APPROXIMATE wording: it covers ANY individual providing personal or professional services, not only \"performers\". It makes a replica clause \"unenforceable\" (the dossier says \"voids\") only when ALL three conditions hold. It applies to new performances fixed on or after 1 Jan 2025.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-16"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U79/G5: California AB 1836 (Ch. 258). A digital replica of a dead performer's voice or likeness in an audiovisual work or sound recording without consent brings liability of the greater of $10,000 or actual damages. \"70 years\" is the window in the existing section 3344.1. The text read gives no explicit effective date: 1 Jan 2025 is California's default for bills like this, not a date read in the text.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-17"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5: NEW YORK, contract law (Gen. Oblig. Law 5-302, S.7676-B, ch. 581, signed 13 Dec 2024, in force 1 Jan 2025). It is the same model as AB 2602, and union-covered work is exempt only if the union contract \"expressly\" addresses replicas. TRAP: the bill as first introduced also covered clauses allowing voice or likeness to be used \"to train\" AI. The ENACTED text does not.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-18"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5: NEW YORK, the dead (Civil Rights Law 50-f, S.8391, ch. 616, signed 11 Dec 2025, effective immediately per the sponsor memo). Using a dead personality's digital replica in an audiovisual work, sound recording or live musical performance without prior consent is actionable, with at least $2,000 in damages. The old safe harbour for a credits disclaimer is deleted.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-19"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5: NEW YORK, ads (Gen. Bus. Law 396-b, S.8420-A, ch. 617, signed 11 Dec 2025). Ads must disclose a \"synthetic performer\" (an AI performer who is NOT a recognisable real person). Penalties are $1,000, then $5,000. It takes effect 180 days after becoming law (sponsor memo), so about June 2026 (my arithmetic). It is a disclosure rule, not a likeness right.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-20"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5: ILLINOIS HB 4875 (Public Act 103-0836, in force 1 Jan 2025). It amends the Right of Publicity Act to cover distributing a recording or audiovisual work containing an unauthorised \"digital replica\", with a host safe harbour. From the legislature's synopsis of Floor Amendment 2; the enrolled text was not read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-21"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5: ILLINOIS HB 4762 (Public Act 103-0830, in force 9 Aug 2024), the Digital Voice and Likeness Protection Act. It is a contract rule like AB 2602. Only the synopsis AS INTRODUCED was read, so its details (e.g. \"retroactively\") may have changed by amendment.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-22"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5: CALIFORNIA 2026, SB 1050 (signed 16 Sept 2026, per the release's URL date). Ads that use AI-generated performers must disclose it. The effective date is not in the release (secondary reports say 1 Jan 2027; unverified). California SB 683 (Oct 2025, fast takedowns for commercial misuse of identity) is from SAG-AFTRA's timeline only and its text was not read. AB 412 / SB 53 (training disclosure / frontier safety) were not researched: neither is a likeness law.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-23"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G5 summary (ledger, F19 \"nothing protects a performer's voice\"). VERDICT: FALSE as a general statement. Enacted state law read at the source: Tennessee (a voice right for everyone, plus liability for clone TOOLS); California (replica-contract rule, dead performers, 2026 ad disclosure); New York (replica-contract rule, dead performers, ad disclosure); Illinois (publicity-right digital replicas, replica-contract rule). That is four states, so \"only two states\" is wrong. BUT the protection has holes: the contract laws bite only on vague clauses with no lawyer or union; no state law read bans TRAINING on a performance (New York dropped that clause, D1-18); NO FAKES is not law (D1-25); the UK and Denmark have not legislated (D1-28 to D1-30). Other states were NOT surveyed, so say \"at least four\", never \"only four\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-24"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U60: NO FAKES (S.4591), at congress.gov. Introduced 20 May 2026; Judiciary ordered it reported favourably 18 June 2026; placed on the calendar 24 June; status \"Introduced\" (not passed). CORRECTED: \"cleared Senate Judiciary by unanimous voice vote\" is NOT in the official action record (it gives no tally). That wording came from the engine's finding or a secondary relay. H.R.8915 was not checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-25"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U60: On 30 Sept 2026 Sen. Cruz objected on the floor to a unanimous-consent request to pass NO FAKES. Secondary (a reprint quoting Politico); the Congressional Record was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-26"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U101: UK, March 2026 report (published 18 March 2026). There is no general personal image right, and the government will only \"explore\" options. Not legislated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-27"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U59: Denmark's draft amends the Copyright Act to protect appearance and voice, and performers' performances. The draft expected a start on 1 July 2026, which has passed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-28"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U59: On 8 Oct 2026 the bill was \"due to be introduced\", with the minister aiming for the start of 2027. Secondary only: the Folketing's page (ft.dk) showed a Cloudflare challenge. Whether it was introduced, its number (\"L 49\" in one blog) and whether it has passed are UNVERIFIED. Say \"a bill, not yet in force\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-29"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U91: Equity (UK), December 2025 INDICATIVE ballot on refusing on-set scanning. ADDITION: the figure is 99.6% Yes (TV and film workers). Equity threatened a statutory ballot if Pact did not move. A 75.1% turnout appeared only in a search snippet (not saved; do not use).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-30"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U91: The indicative ballot was NOT binding and was not a strike vote. Equity's own unmet demand was TRAINING. Status after Jan 2026 (deal, statutory ballot or strike) is UNRESOLVED: one search found nothing later, and equity.org.uk was not reachable through the search tool.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d1-31"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U4/F9: Playtika's CEO letter (SEC exhibit): about a 15% cut; \"teams powered by AI and automation\"; AND \"the economic reality of our industry has shifted\". No replaced jobs are named. The 14 Jan 2026 date and the ~500-525 headcount were NOT checked here (the letter is undated).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-32"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U5/F9: GDC 2026 survey. More than 2,300 respondents; 28% laid off in two years (33% in the US); 36% use gen AI at work; 52% say gen AI is hurting the industry (30%, then 18%, in earlier years). The organiser's release reports no question on what CAUSED the layoffs (that is what the release shows; the full report was not read). FilmLA's 19,694 shoot days and EA's \"augmentation\" line were NOT opened (UNFINISHED).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-33"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U37: Square Enix aims to automate 70% of QA and debugging by end-2027 with gen AI. Its Western layoffs were reported the same day, with no official statement. Secondary (80.lv); the company's progress-report PDF was not opened. The president's \"restructuring\" words are a paraphrase prefixed \"Purportedly\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-34"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U38: Larian. CORRECTED: the studio head DISPUTES Bloomberg's paraphrase that Larian uses AI to \"develop concept art\". He says AI is used to \"explore\" references and that artists develop the concept art. The \"no AI components\" line was said to IGN. 72 artists, 23 of them concept artists, and hiring: self-reported. The error came from a secondary relay (Bloomberg's wording, carried into the engine finding).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-35"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U87/F9: Indie Game Awards. The statement was posted 2025-12-20. The reason given: the studio agreed at submission that no gen AI was used, which was not the case. ADDITIONS: an Indie Vanguard recipient was also retracted, and the awards went to Blue Prince and Sorry We're Closed. The claims that the rule covers \"any stage\" and that the assets were \"patched out\" were NOT found in the saved text. The IGA's own FAQ renders by script and could not be read (UNRESOLVED parts).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-36"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U47: The ALS voice-clone case and its company ties. The speech therapist is also an ElevenLabs consultant, and the performance was at ElevenLabs' own London summit on 11 Feb. The musician's own words are confirmed. \"Impact programme run by a speech therapist\" was NOT found (the article says only that ElevenLabs launched an impact programme). Name roles on air, not the private individual.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-37"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F9 ledger claim \"studios are firing artists and replacing them with AI\". VERDICT: MOSTLY NOT MEASURED. Layoffs are measured (D1-33), but no source read ties a cut of ARTISTS to AI. The one employer that itself links a cut to AI is Playtika, a mobile-games company, and it names no roles and gives a second, economic reason (D1-32). Square Enix's AI plan targets QA, not art, and the link is reported, not stated (D1-34). Larian denies replacing artists (D1-35). Comparator: the same survey's 52% \"AI is bad for the industry\" is an opinion measure, not a job-loss measure.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d1-38"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U81/U15: ElevenLabs says Voice Library creators have earned over $22M, double the $11M of Nov 2025, with 10,400+ creators earning. Vendor claim, no audit, no per-person breakdown. Post dated 22 May 2026. Comparator: $22M / 10,400 = about $2,100 per creator over the platform's whole life, a mean that the post's own star examples (a voice actor earning \"a full-time living\") skew upward.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U16: per-use rate. Base $0.03 per 1,000 characters, up to $0.20 for HQ/rare voices; a 10-minute script earns about $0.27; the Reddit and blogger earnings are anecdotes the vendor chose. Getting a professional clone listed needs the $22/month Creator Plan. Trap: the same page (last updated Oct 2026) still carries stale totals ($5M, \"over $1 million\"), so never quote this page for the total.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U80: Adobe's 2025 Firefly bonus (its third) was announced 17 Sept 2025 and based on content considered for training from 3 June 2024 to 2 June 2025 plus the licences it generated. No formula or total is published in the announcement. $4.88 is ONE contributor's reported banner; a community expert (not Adobe staff) says bonuses of $1 or less are not paid.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U65: HarperCollins' offer is $5,000 per title split 50-50 (so $2,500 to the author), opt-in, with an output cap of 200 consecutive words / 5%; the Authors Guild says the split favours the publisher. NOT in this source: \"Microsoft\" and the \"3-year term\". The Guild calls the licensee \"an unknown AI company\"; the Microsoft identification comes from press reports not read here. Name the buyer only with that caveat.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G12 (Bartz v. Anthropic payout, primary = the claims administrator's site): $1.5B fund, about $3,000 per WORK (not per person), before fees (counsel asked for up to 25%) and costs; the default split is 50% to authors, 50% to publishers. Comparator: an author sharing a trade book with a publisher gets about $1,500 before fees. This is a lawsuit settlement, not a licensing deal: don't count it as \"licensing pays\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G12 (Bartz status): final approval 20 July 2026; claims closed 30 March 2026. As of today (2 Nov 2026) NO payment has gone out: the first round is expected on or before 15 Nov 2026, and works under dispute between co-claimants wait longer.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F23 (Getty): over $220M in royalties in 2025, from all licensing; the AI-training share is not broken out. Getty says its generative AI partnerships pay creators \"on a recurring basis\" but gives no amount. Don't divide $220M by the 83,000 exclusive contributors: royalties also go to content partners.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F23 (Shutterstock): the $203.3M is NOT data licensing. It is the 10-K's \"Data, Distribution, and Services\" revenue line, which grew mainly on metadata sales plus distribution and services, and it is Shutterstock's revenue, not money paid to contributors. No contributor-fund total is in the 10-K text I searched. Right wording: \"Shutterstock's data, distribution and services revenue was $203.3M in 2025; it doesn't say how much reached contributors.\" Error source: engine finding (relabelled the line).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U102 (UMG-Udio, Oct 2025): financial terms undisclosed; opt-in artists to be paid for training use and when fans create with their work. Secondary-verified (trade relay of UMG's statement; UMG's own release not read).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U102 (WMG-Suno, 25 Nov 2025): \"full control\" over name, image, likeness, voice, compositions; the current models are to be deprecated in 2026 and downloads limited to paid accounts. No sums. Secondary-verified (MBW quoting the release).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F12: \"the labels settled with Suno and Udio\". WRONG as a blanket statement. Udio has deals with UMG and Warner; Suno has a deal with Warner ONLY. UMG and Sony are still suing Suno, and Sony is still suing Udio (a second suit was filed 20 July 2026 over 30,117 recordings, after a 29 June ruling). Right wording: \"Two of the three majors have licensed Udio, one has licensed Suno, and Sony has settled with neither.\" Error source: engine finding (\"UMG and Warner have deals\" left out that UMG is still suing Suno).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U102: \"UMG/Sony talks with Suno hit an impasse in April 2026 over letting generated tracks leave the platform.\" The FT (via MBW, 9 Apr 2026) says the majors \"reject its model for distributing AI-generated music\". \"Leave the platform\" is a gloss from other relays, close but not the FT's words. The same report says Warner's own Suno partnership \"has seen minimal progress\". Secondary-verified.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G12 (are Suno deal terms public?): no. They are withheld even from the co-plaintiff labels: a magistrate blocked UMG and Sony from obtaining the Warner-Suno settlement agreement (Apr 2026). Secondary-verified (RightsTech brief).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U36/F12 (Spotify remix tool): licensed but not launched. Per the FT (via Music Ally, 30 Oct 2026): targeting early 2027 with a possible Grammys reveal, a $4-5/month add-on, Sony and Warner still negotiating. This relay lists UMG, Merlin and Believe as licensors (Kobalt is a publisher deal, D2-15). The Believe date (20 Oct) was not verified. Financial terms undisclosed. Secondary-verified.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U36: Spotify's Kobalt deal (13 Aug 2026) followed Merlin (4 Aug); the tool is to be a paid Premium add-on; Spotify's line is \"consent, credit, and compensation\", with no rates. Secondary-verified.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U93 (STIM): a collective AI licence; pilot with Songfox AND Sureel (Sureel is a launch partner, not only the attribution vendor); only a limited set of opted-in works; royalties are promised at training, use and output stages; no rates published. Date: STIM's own page says 8 Sep 2025, CISAC says 9 Sep 2025; say \"September 2025\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-16"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F13/U43 (Disney-OpenAI, as announced 11 Dec 2025, Disney's own release): a three-year licence of 200+ characters to Sora; Disney to make a $1B equity investment plus warrants; no talent likenesses or voices; subject to definitive agreements and closing conditions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-17"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U43: \"Disney was to invest, not collect a fee; the money flowed only to OpenAI.\" WRONG as stated. Disney's CEO said on the day that \"there's a license fee associated with it\"; the amount was never disclosed. Right wording: \"Disney was to invest $1B in OpenAI and, its CEO said, receive a licence fee of undisclosed size.\" Error source: engine finding (an inference from the release, which doesn't mention a fee). Secondary-verified (CNN via an affiliate).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-18"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U43/F13: the deal collapsed when OpenAI shut Sora down (late March 2026). It reportedly never closed and no money changed hands. The ONLY text read is a fan blog relaying Reuters' anonymous sources, so this is secondary-verified at best: say \"reportedly\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-19"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U23/F15 (Glaze's own evaluation, arXiv 2302.04222): above 92% normal and above 85% adaptive, at p=0.05, from author-run artist ratings plus CLIP scores; 1,156 artists in the first survey. CORRECTION: the \"94.3%\" is NOT an artist-rated success rate. In the paper it is the share of artists willing to post art at the lower perturbation p=0.03. Don't quote 94.3% as protection. Error source: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-20"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G12 (Glaze adoption): the only figure found is the authors' own count, more than 740K downloads as of June 2023. Downloads are not users, and there's no later figure on the project's pages (home, what-is, FAQ). No Nightshade adoption figure found.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-21"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U24 (Hönig, Rando, Carlini, Tramèr, arXiv 2406.12027v2): off-the-shelf \"robust mimicry\" (noisy upscaling best) beats Glaze, Mist and Anti-DreamBooth. 10 artists (5 historical, 5 contemporary), MTurk annotators, disclosed before publication; Glaze shipped v2.1 in response. Their test of Glaze 2.0 found noisy upscaling \"still achieves almost perfect style mimicry\". Nightshade does not appear in the paper's text (0 hits). Venue: none on the arXiv page (ICLR 2025 unverified).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-22"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U107 (the project's own caveats, what-is page): not a permanent solution; weaker or useless for styles already in base models; two known attacks (IMPRESS, noisy upscaler), and v2.1 was updated against the latter.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-23"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U107 (img2img, the project's FAQ): Glaze was designed for style mimicry, not img2img, and the team does not believe it gives consistent img2img protection. Note the tension: the same FAQ calls the cloaks robust to \"denoising\", while the Hönig paper removed them with noise-then-upscale.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-24"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U25 (Nightshade evidence is lab-only): the abstract says \"less than 100 poisoned training samples\" on SDXL, not \"fewer than 100 poison samples\". Same meaning, but quote the paper's words. This is the authors' own lab result; no real-world training run is reported.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-25"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U25 (Nightshade's own FAQ): most effective on Stable Diffusion; works only on diffusion models and only when a lot of shaded images are trained on. \"No model developer has reported being affected\": no such report found in this pass. That's an absence of evidence, not a verified negative, and it cannot be quoted.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-26"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U51 (LightShed, primary = the USENIX Security 2025 paper): the 99.98% is a TRUE-POSITIVE RATE (with 100% true-negative rate) for DETECTING Nightshade-poisoned images, not a general \"accuracy\", and not a removal rate. Right wording: \"detects 99.98% of Nightshade-poisoned images in the authors' tests\". Error source: engine finding / secondary relay.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-27"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U51 (the reply from the tools' co-creator, via The Register): \"turn up the knob on randomization\"; LightShed is weaker on poisons it hasn't seen; code shared with the Glaze team but not published.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-28"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U19/F24 (Axis, grunt gallery, BC, 2026): 26 completed surveys of 100 invited disabled/Deaf artists; over 90% use AI; accessibility tools about 70-85%, creative/generative tools about 20-35%; human services unavailable 85%, too expensive 77%; only 8% say AI is better than human services. On the \"80% fear cuts\" figure: the concern chart lists \"AI used to cut human services\" at 80%, and the text says about 70-80%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-29"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U75: \"the peer-reviewed research...\" is WRONG for the first example. The 6-blind-artists Midjourney study is an arXiv preprint with no venue listed. Right wording: \"small interview studies, some peer-reviewed (ASSETS 2024/2025), one a preprint\". Error source: engine finding.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-30"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U75: Bennett et al. (Google Research authors, ASSETS '24): 10 disabled creatives who use AND who don't use generative AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-31"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U75: Hayward et al. (ASSETS '25): 5 disabled visual artists, GenAI for business and admin work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-32"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U75: Cossovich, Wu, Girouard (ASSETS 2024): 15 interviews with artists with upper-limb motor impairments. AI is a minor theme: participants imagined AI-powered tools. The \"AI help they could accept or reject\" wording was not found in this read; use \"imagined AI tools\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-33"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U92 (ElevenLabs Impact Program, 20 Aug 2024): the headline is a target (\"1 million\"); free licences via Bridging Voice and the Scott-Morgan Foundation; no count of people served on the page; the 300,000 / 95% figures are stated without a source.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-34"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F24 (the ALS musician case, MIT Technology Review 13 Feb 2026): the vendor ties are real. The speech therapist who introduced the tool is an ElevenLabs consultant; the performance was at ElevenLabs' own summit (11 Feb); the musician said the voice \"feels like a different version of me\". Name the role, not the person.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-35"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U103 (NaNoWriMo, Sept 2024): its unattributed site statement said condemning AI outright ignores \"classist and ableist issues\"; writers left its board.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-36"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"U103: \"closed in April 2025 (finances plus the AI fight)\". The shutdown was announced by email around 31 Mar / 1 Apr 2025 (Lit Hub, 1 Apr 2025). The stated causes were money and participation problems, made worse by the volunteer/moderator safety scandal. The AI stance is a press-attributed contributing \"misstep\", not the organisation's stated reason. Right wording: \"it shut down in spring 2025, citing money; the AI row was one of several controversies.\" Error source: engine finding (compressed the cause).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-37"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G6 (AI-company deals with publishers): the one widely quoted figure, News Corp-OpenAI \"more than $250M over five years\", is NOT disclosed by either company. It is the WSJ (a News Corp paper) citing sources, and it includes OpenAI credits, not just cash. It pays the publisher; nothing shows what reaches journalists. The AP, FT and Axel Springer values were not checked (UNFINISHED). Secondary-verified.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-38"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G6 (Spotify's own numbers): over 75M \"spammy tracks\" removed in 12 months (to Sept 2025), and total payouts of $10B in 2024 (vendor claims). Spam removal is not an AI-licensing payment, and \"spammy\" is not \"AI-generated\". Comparator only: ElevenLabs' whole-lifetime creator payout ($22M) is about 0.2% of one year of Spotify music payouts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-39"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"G8 (is \"don't tag your work\" what people repeat?): only partly documented. ep49's council card (viral point 2) records \"Glaze / Nightshade, 'don't tag your work'\" as a harvested cluster of about 28,600 likes over 25 comments, and says the likes are approximate cluster sums. That is a harvest count, not an export of ep49 viewer comments, and no record I read gives the comment text, so what \"tag\" means (metadata, hashtags, AI labels) is still unknown. The same card lists the Suno/Udio deals, the Disney character licence and disabled creators as UNADDRESSED viral points without counts. To close: export the harvested comments behind that cluster and quote them.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d2-40"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"F23/G12 (overall: do licensing deals pay creators?): the one platform with a disclosed per-use rate pays $0.03 per 1,000 characters. Every other music, book and image deal in this pass publishes no per-creator rate (D2-3, D2-7 to D2-16). See the ledger verdicts below.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-41"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 1 (Miyazaki): the remark was broadcast in NHK's documentary \"NHKスペシャル 終わらない人 宮崎駿\" (in English, \"Never-Ending Man\"), aired 13 Nov 2016. The Japanese words are 極めて何か生命に対する侮辱を感じます, which is roughly \"I feel, extremely, something like an insult to life itself\". The familiar English line, \"I strongly feel that this is an insult to life itself\", is a subtitle translation and is not checked here. Secondary-verified: NHK's broadcast is not readable as text.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 1: what he was responding to. A Dwango CG team, led by its chairman, showed a figure whose movement had been learned by AI. It moved by using its head as a leg, and the pitch was that the motion suited zombie games. Miyazaki answered by describing a friend with a physical disability who finds even a high-five hard. The demo was motion learning (reinforcement-style locomotion), not image generation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 1: the fuller remark, from J-CAST's 2024 transcription of the same scene. He says the makers think nothing of pain, calls it extremely unpleasant, and says he has no wish to connect it to his own work. The two J-CAST transcriptions differ slightly (何か vs なにか, and the presenter's words), so neither is NHK's own text. A script should quote the short line and say \"in a 2016 NHK documentary\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 1: \"Miyazaki says AI art/AI image generators are an insult to life itself.\" CORRECTED. The remark is from 2016, before public image generators existed, and was about one demo of AI-learned movement. The presenter called it \"almost an experiment\". In September 2024 he said on X that he wished the clip's recirculation would stop. Right wording: \"In 2016, shown an AI animation demo, Miyazaki called it 'an insult to life itself' and said he wanted nothing to do with it in his work.\" This pass found no Miyazaki or Studio Ghibli statement on image generators (not searched exhaustively; the October 2025 industry request to OpenAI on Sora was not opened). Error source: secondary relay, the viral recirculation of 2023-25.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2 (97%): the sample. Deezer commissioned the survey; Ipsos ran it. It was online, 9,000 adults aged 18-65, in 8 countries (US, Canada, Brazil, UK, France, Netherlands, Germany, Japan), fielded 6-10 Oct 2025, with national quotas. Released 12 Nov 2025. No Ipsos report or topline of its own was found: the methodology note and figures are Deezer's release only. Deezer is an interested party: it sells an AI-music detector (hand pass B).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2: the task and what \"97%\" means. Each respondent heard three tracks (two fully AI, one human) and judged whether each was fully AI-generated. \"97% failed\" means 97% did not get all three right; only 3% did. It does NOT mean 97% judged no better than a guess on a track. Per-track accuracy, the exact question wording, and whether people were told the 2:1 mix are not published. Our arithmetic: pure guessing on three yes/no calls gets all three right 1 time in 8 (12.5%), so 3% is BELOW chance. That fits respondents leaning toward \"human\" on the AI tracks, but the release doesn't show it. Ledger: PARTLY TRUE. Say \"in Deezer's test, only 3 in 100 got all three tracks right\", never \"97% can't tell AI music from human music\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2: the attitude results from the same survey, all respondents unless marked. 71% surprised, 52% uncomfortable, 80% want 100%-AI music labelled. Among streaming users (n=6,791), 66% would try AI music once and 40% would skip it unheard.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2 conflict, part 1: \"34% / 50,000 a day\" is Deezer's 12 Nov 2025 figure, published in the same release as the survey. The release gives it two ways: \"34%\" in the summary and \"more than 34%\" in the body. These are uploads (deliveries), not streams; a relay (Gigazine) misreported it as streams.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2 conflict, part 2 (the middle point, not in the harvest): 29 Jan 2026, about 60,000 a day, about 39%, averaged over January 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2 conflict, part 3: \"44% / 75,000 a day\" is Deezer's 20 Apr 2026 figure. Deezer says the daily count rose from 10,000 to 75,000 in little over a year. So there is no conflict: these are successive releases at one service. In order: 10,000 (Jan 2025) → 18% (Apr 2025) → 30,000/28% (Sept 2025) → 50,000/34% (Nov 2025) → 60,000/39% (Jan 2026) → 75,000/44% (Apr 2026) → nearly 90,000/more than 50% on peak days (June 2026, B rows). The harvest's \"conflict\" comes from comparing two different dates.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 2 trap: the fraud and listening shares also move between releases. The Nov 2025 release says up to 70% of streams on AI tracks are fraudulent and AI tracks are about 0.5% of streams. Jan/Apr 2026 say up to 85% for 2025 and 1-3% of streams. Each figure belongs to its own release date; never mix them in one sentence.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 3 (strike): what the union said the strike was about when it called it (release dated 25 July 2024; strike from 26 July). Agreement had been reached on many issues; the sticking point was AI language covering all performers. (Strike vote 98.32%, Sept 2023; already D1.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 3: mid-strike, 11 Mar 2025. The union's member message says the remaining gap was AI: past performances, consent, payment, replicas working during a future strike, and usage reporting. The only \"hiring\" matter in it is the union warning members not to fill struck roles, which it frames as an AI-protection issue. Nothing about dues or union security.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 3: at the end. On 10 June 2025 the AP described the tentative deal as ending a strike that halted over AI protections. The union's chief negotiator called the deal AI guardrails \"alongside other important gains\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Harvest 3: \"it stopped being about AI; it was really about dues, closed shop and control of hiring\". CORRECTED; ledger FALSE. The union's statements at the start, middle and end, the AP's report, and the employers' own spokesperson at ratification all describe the result in terms of wages, AI and safety. No document opened, and neither of two searches, shows any dispute over dues, union-security (closed or union shop) clauses, or hiring control. Limits: absence in the record, not proof of absence. The full contract summary was not readable (see Method), and the employers' April/May 2025 \"final offer\" was read only through secondary relays. Right wording: \"Both sides described the deal in terms of AI, wages and safety; no record shows a fight over dues.\" Error source: comment harvest, unsourced.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1 (what a placeholder is): an industry primary. Unity's own blog (11 May 2026) describes the standard practice: build with grey boxes and stand-in \"placeholder art\" first, final art later, so that art isn't wasted on things that get cut. A placeholder is by definition a stand-in meant to be replaced before release. Fetched from https://unity.com/blog/placeholder-asset-problem","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-1"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1 (was it shipped, then patched): the game went on sale 24 April 2025 (Steam store page, fetched https://store.steampowered.com/app/1903340/). On 2 Nov 2026 the page shows no AI-content disclosure section. That shows only what is there today, not whether one was ever filed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-2"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1: the studio's own patch note. Hotfix 1.2.2, posted 29 Apr 2025, five days after release (our arithmetic): \"Replaced a placeholder texture with the correct visual asset\". The note does not say AI. That the replaced texture was the AI one comes from the press (T2-5) and the director's later words (T2-6). A second note (Patch 1.3.0, 10 June 2025) removed \"placeholder textures\" elsewhere. Nothing links those to AI, so don't air them as AI. Fetched from Valve's news API for app 1903340 (URL in Method).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-3"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1 (what the award body said; the declaration at submission): the full statement, as relayed by Push Square (22 Dec 2025). Its own primaries could not be read (see Method; D1-36 has the VGC relay and the 20 Dec date). Two points matter for the script. The declaration was made at submission by a studio representative. The trigger was the studio \"confirming the use of gen AI art in production\" on the day of the premiere (18 Dec 2025). Secondary-verified. Fetched from https://www.pushsquare.com/news/2025/12/ps5-fave-expedition-33-stripped-of-indie-game-awards-but-not-for-the-reasons-you-may-think","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-4"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1: the link between the AI textures and the patch, and the studio's earlier words. PC Gamer (21 Dec 2025) says fans spotted seemingly AI-made textures days after the April release. They were removed soon after, under a patch note calling them a placeholder texture. In June 2025 a co-founder told El País the studio used \"some AI, but not much\"; the article was later updated to say no element in the game is made with gen AI. The awards went to Blue Prince and Sorry We're Closed (already D1-36). Fetched from https://www.pcgamer.com/games/rpg/indie-game-awards-pulls-two-awards-from-clair-obscur-over-generative-ai-use-we-have-a-hard-stance-against-gen-ai-in-videogames/","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-5"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1: the studio's own account after the retraction, from the director in a YouTube interview, relayed by GamesRadar (24 Dec 2025). The team tried AI in 2022, used it as placeholders for textures it missed, and took it out once found. He says \"everything in the game is human made\", naming concept art and voice work, and that the studio won't use such tools in future. Secondary-verified (the interview is video). This is the studio's self-report; no outside audit of the asset pipeline exists. Fetched from https://www.gamesradar.com/games/rpg/clair-obscur-expedition-33-director-admits-sandfall-tried-ai-during-the-jrpgs-development-but-didnt-like-it-and-everything-in-the-game-is-human-made/","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-6"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1 (what disclosure rules exist): Steam's content-survey rules, undated on the page; secondaries date this wording to Jan 2026. Developers must disclose AI-made content \"that ships with your game and is consumed by players\". Behind-the-scenes efficiency tools are out of scope. Our reading: an AI texture that ships, even by mistake, is the kind of content the rule covers. Whether the game filed a disclosure while the texture shipped (April 2025, under the earlier wording) is not knowable from what we read. Fetched from https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/gettingstarted/contentsurvey","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-7"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q1 VERDICT. \"AI placeholders are a non-issue\": PARTLY TRUE. True: by every account, AI was used only for a few stand-in textures, which a hotfix replaced five days after launch. False: \"placeholder\" did not keep it off players' screens (it shipped), and under the award's rule, which the studio's representative had signed off at submission, any gen-AI use disqualified the game. The award was pulled over the false declaration, not over the textures' size. Mirror, \"the game was made with AI\": PARTLY TRUE, as L36 already stamps it: AI touched the making (\"gen AI was used in the making\", the award body's words), but no source shows AI-made content in the game players have today. The committee's spoken words are relayed by PC Gamer from the video.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-8"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 (Spotify's own total, latest year): more than $11B paid to the music industry in 2025, nearly $70B lifetime (Loud & Clear 2026, published March 2026). It follows the $10B for 2024 (B27). This is the vendor's own figure. Fetched from https://loudandclear.byspotify.com/takeaways/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-9"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 (the share paid out; who gets paid; no per-stream rate): Spotify says it pays out about two-thirds of music revenue, and pays rights holders (labels, distributors, publishers, collecting societies), not artists. It says no service pays a fixed per-stream amount: a \"per-stream rate\" is total payouts divided by total streams, worked out afterwards. It gives its own measure instead: 1 in every million streams in 2025 earned about $11,000. Fetched from https://loudandclear.byspotify.com/faq/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-10"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 (the 1,000-stream minimum; Spotify's own announcement, 21 Nov 2023, live from 1 April 2024): tracks need 1,000 streams in the previous 12 months to earn recording royalties. Spotify's reason: tens of millions of tracks averaged $0.03 a month, often stuck below distributors' withdrawal minimums. Together that was about $40M a year, now shared among the other tracks. Spotify's FAQ says the policy changes neither the pool nor its profit (the vendor's claim, not checked). Fetched from https://artists.spotify.com/en/blog/modernizing-our-royalty-system","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-11"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 (the regulator's split; UK CMA final report, 29 Nov 2022, paragraph 5.101, figures for 2021, from the largest services' data): of streaming revenue, recording rights (labels) took over 50%, publishing (songwriters and publishers) 15%, and the services kept 32% (down from 41% in 2008). So \"the labels take the rest\" is wrong as said: the service keeps about a third. That fits Spotify's \"two-thirds\" (T2-10). Fetched from https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6384f43ee90e077898ccb48e/Musicandstreamingfinalreport.pdf","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-12"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 (what reaches the artist; CMA, UK artists signed to the three majors): the average gross royalty on new multi-track contracts rose from 19.7% (2012) to 23.3% (2021). Across all UK artists at the majors, earnings before recoupment were 26.3% of the majors' UK streaming revenue in 2021, and the average artist earned about £2,000 a year. Our arithmetic, not the CMA's: about a quarter of a recording share just over half means roughly 13-14p of each £1 of streaming revenue reaches a UK major-label artist, before advances and costs are recouped. DIY artists through distributors are on different terms, not measured here.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-13"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 (the regulator's reading): the money is concentrated. In IPO research cited by the CMA, the top 0.4% of artists took 63-65% of streams in 2014-2020. The CMA said competition policy can't fix the split; that is for government. Its broader finding (from the executive summary relays, not quoted here) was that artists' problems are not caused by weak competition.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-14"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q2 VERDICT. \"Spotify pays pennies; the labels take the rest\": PARTLY TRUE. \"Pennies per stream\": NOT MEASURED as a rate. Spotify sets no per-stream price (T2-10). Every per-stream figure in circulation is someone dividing payouts by streams, so per-stream averages are third-party estimates; none was opened here. \"The labels take the rest\": CORRECTED. The service keeps about a third (32% in the CMA's 2021 UK data). The labels take over half of all streaming revenue. On a major-label deal the label keeps roughly three-quarters of the recording money before the artist's share, which is itself before recoupment (T2-12, T2-13). Error source: the claim's own wording. Right wording: \"Spotify keeps about a third; of the rest, labels take the biggest slice, and a typical UK major-label artist gets about a quarter of the label's share.\"","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-15"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3a (which AI acts reached a Billboard chart, and which charts; Billboard, 4 Nov 2025, updated to the 15 Nov 2025 charts): at least six AI or AI-assisted acts in a few months. Billboard identifies them from the acts' own pages, from Deezer's detector, and by asking the creators. The charts named are genre and sales charts: Christian, Rock and Country Digital Song Sales, Emerging Artists, Hot Gospel Songs and Hot R&B Songs. One airplay chart: Xania Monet debuted at No. 30 on Adult R&B Airplay, the first known AI act on a Billboard radio chart, and she topped R&B Digital Song Sales. Most debuts came on about 1,000 downloads. Monet is \"AI-assisted\" in Billboard's sense: a human writes the lyrics and Suno makes the music. Fetched from https://www.billboard.com/lists/ai-artists-on-billboard-charts/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-16"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3a (the No. 1): Breaking Rust's \"Walk My Walk\" was No. 1 on Country Digital Song Sales (paid downloads only) for a second week on the 15 Nov 2025 chart. It sold just over 2,000 US copies; the all-genre download leader that week sold 29,000. Country radio had not added it. Billboard Pro, 13 Nov 2025. Fetched from https://www.billboard.com/pro/ai-artists-breaking-rust-country-music-chart-reactions/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-17"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3a: Billboard's own year-end framing (11 Dec 2025) is \"smaller Billboard charts\". The viral \"I Run\" reached Spotify's charts, not a Billboard one, in this text. No Billboard page read shows an AI act on the Hot 100. That is an absence, not a check of every chart week to Nov 2026; reports that Billboard withheld \"I Run\" from the Hot 100 were not opened. Fetched from https://www.billboard.com/lists/biggest-ai-music-stories-2025-suno-udio-charts-more/ai-songs-hit-the-charts/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-18"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3a (any Billboard policy on AI): no Billboard policy document was found. Billboard labels and counts AI acts (T2-16) and has not been shown to exclude them. In July 2026 the three majors and several indies proposed chart rules: AI-assisted (\"substantially human made\") tracks can chart, fully AI-generated ones can't, and AI use must be declared. That is a proposal, relayed by trade press (CMU, 29 Jul 2026); the labels' own document was not opened. Australia's chart body adopted a similar exclusion (search snippets only, not opened: don't air). Fetched from https://completemusicupdate.com/record-labels-come-together-to-propose-rules-for-when-ai-generated-tracks-can-get-into-the-music-charts/","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-19"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3b (what SAG-AFTRA said; the union's statement of 30 Sep 2025, secondary-verified): sagaftra.org/sag-aftra-statement-synthetic-performer returns 403 direct, on archive.org and on r.jina.ai. TheWrap and AV Club quote it identically. Points: the union opposes replacing human performers with \"synthetics\"; the character was \"trained on the work of countless professional performers\" without permission or pay; signatory producers must give notice and bargain before using a synthetic performer. Fetched from https://www.thewrap.com/ai-actors-in-hollywood-sag-aftra-opposes-tilly-norwood/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-20"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3b (who made it; the agency claim): made by Particle 6, the creator's production studio, with its AI talent studio (Xicoia; Global News spells it \"Xiocia\"). In Sept 2025 the creator said an agency would be announced \"in the next few months\". Global News, 2 Mar 2026, quoting the creator's earlier words. Fetched from https://globalnews.ca/news/11713239/ai-actor-tilly-norwood-tillyverse-eline-van-der-velden-xiocia/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-21"},{"s":"ep54-ai-creative-jobs-claims","n":54,"c":"Q3b VERDICT (the record by Jul 2026): no agency signing is on record. Variety (6 Jul 2026) still calls it \"claims\" that she was about to be signed. Her first feature, \"Misaligned\", was announced by her own studio, is in early development, and the studio itself says it needs human directors, writers and editors. So \"an AI actress got signed\": NOT MEASURED (claimed, never shown; no source read says outright that no agency signed her). \"An AI actress is taking roles\": PARTLY TRUE (one announced lead, in her maker's own film). Fetched from https://variety.com/2026/film/global/ai-actor-tilly-norwood-movie-debut-misaligned-1236802325/","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-22"}]);